oracle:
  verdict: "pass"
  statement: >
    Independent read-only review recomputed exact cosine from fixture bytes for
    all 36 queries, parsed and hashed all four compact graph artifacts, verified
    that every graph contained the identical vector bytes, recomputed all twelve
    receipt gates, and found no result-invalidating defect.
decision: >
  Keep exact brute-force dense retrieval as the immediate Semble baseline
  default. HNSW reduced the measured dense component from 4.560 ms to 0.538 ms
  at ef=400, but added 46.981 seconds to a cold four-worker baseline and removed
  only about four milliseconds from a 56.689-ms hybrid query. A fresh graph
  would require roughly 11,700 searches to amortize its construction cost.
  Retain the merged harness and manual workflow because HNSW is technically
  qualified for larger or extensively reused baselines.
revisit_when:
  - "A baseline approaches or exceeds one million vectors."
  - "Exact dense p95 materially consumes the semantic-search latency budget."
  - "One commit-keyed graph can be reused across enough jobs to amortize construction."
  - "A unified multi-repository index is proposed."
  - "Concurrent exact scans create measured memory-bandwidth pressure."
  - "A new builder materially reduces complete HNSW construction time."
residual: >
  The witness covers one r3-32 execution and one static corpus. Parallel graph
  edge order is scheduler-dependent. HNSW latency was warmed Rust mmap search,
  while the exact-dense control was single-shot Python/NumPy; reported speedup
  is therefore component-scale evidence, not an end-to-end latency claim. The
  experiment did not exercise live delta ingestion.
references:
  - "https://github.com/cleverunicornz/fast-hnsw-fork/pull/2"
